{
  "analysis": "The user cannot curl example.com and wants to know why and how to fix it. The terminal is at a fresh prompt in /app. I need to investigate: is curl installed? Is there a network/DNS/proxy issue? Is there a firewall or DNS misconfiguration?",
  "plan": "First, check if curl is installed and what happens when running it. Then diagnose connectivity: DNS resolution, gateway, proxy environment variables. I'll run a few diagnostic commands to pinpoint the cause.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "which curl; curl --version\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "curl -v example.com\n",
      "duration": 5.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "curl is at /usr/bin/curl, but `curl --version` itself printed 'DNS resolution error' - that's abnormal. `curl --version` should NOT do DNS resolution, so /usr/bin/curl is likely a wrapper script that always errors, not the real curl binary. Also the verbose curl failed with the same DNS error. I need to inspect what /usr/bin/curl actually is.",
  "plan": "Inspect /usr/bin/curl to see if it's a script. Check file type, read it. Also check DNS config (/etc/resolv.conf), environment proxy variables, and whether the real curl binary exists elsewhere. The patch history hints at earlier messing with curl/apt, so something may have replaced the binary.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "file /usr/bin/curl; head -c 200 /usr/bin/curl | cat -v; echo\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat /etc/resolv.conf 2>/dev/null; echo '---'; env | grep -i proxy\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Found the root cause: /usr/bin/curl is NOT the real curl binary — it's a fake wrapper shell script that just prints 'DNS resolution error' and exits 11. That's why even `curl --version` fails. DNS config (resolv.conf) is fine (Docker's 127.0.0.11 resolver). The real curl was overwritten by this stub. I need to restore the real curl binary. Let me look for a cached .deb package or reinstall via apt.",
  "plan": "Check the apt cache for a curl .deb that could be extracted, and check if the real curl exists anywhere else on the system. If there's a .deb, extract the binary from it; otherwise reinstall curl via apt-get. First, investigate what's available.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la /var/cache/apt/archives/ 2>/dev/null | grep -i curl; echo '---'; ls -la /usr/bin/curl\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "dpkg -l | grep -i curl; echo '---'; dpkg -S /usr/bin/curl 2>/dev/null\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "find / -xdev -name 'curl' -type f 2>/dev/null; echo '---'; ls -la /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libcurl* 2>/dev/null\n",
      "duration": 2.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Root cause confirmed: /usr/bin/curl is a 46-byte fake shell script that unconditionally prints 'DNS resolution error' and exits 11. It's not a real curl binary, and no real curl exists anywhere on the system (find only returns the fake /usr/bin/curl). dpkg shows curl isn't installed as a package. The DNS resolver (127.0.0.11) and network config are actually fine — the problem is purely that the curl executable was replaced by this stub. The fix is to restore a real curl binary by (re)installing the curl package via apt.",
  "plan": "Verify apt is available, run apt-get update, then install the real curl package. This will overwrite the fake /usr/bin/curl with the genuine binary. Then test with `curl -I example.com` (or at least `curl --version`) to confirm the fix. If apt has no network, I'll fall back to extracting from a cached .deb or other means.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "which apt-get dpkg; head -n 2 /etc/os-release\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "apt-get update 2>&1 | tail -n 5\n",
      "duration": 20.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "apt-get update succeeded, so the package mirror is reachable and there's no real network/DNS problem — confirming the only issue is the fake /usr/bin/curl script. Now I'll install the real curl package via apt-get, which will overwrite the stub with the genuine binary.",
  "plan": "Run `apt-get install -y curl` and observe output. Then verify with `curl --version` (which should now print real version info, not the DNS error) and a quick `curl -I example.com` test.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "apt-get install -y curl 2>&1 | tail -n 15\n",
      "duration": 30.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "apt-get install failed: 'You don't have enough free space in /var/cache/apt/archives/'. So disk space is the blocker for installing the real curl. The root cause of the curl problem is the fake /usr/bin/curl stub, and the fix requires installing the real curl package, but disk is full. I need to free up space first. The patch history mentioned a test file /var/cache/apt/archives/_test_write and large files. Let me check disk usage and clean up.",
  "plan": "Check disk free space with `df -h`. Look for large files / caches to clean (apt archives, logs, the test file). Clean apt cache and any large temp/test files, then retry the install.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "df -h / /var /tmp 2>/dev/null\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "du -sh /var/cache/apt/archives 2>/dev/null; ls -la /var/cache/apt/archives\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "du -sh /var/log /tmp /root /home /opt /usr/local 2>/dev/null\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Disk is 100% full (0 Available) on the overlay FS, yet the directories I checked are tiny. The apt archives only use 8K. So the space is consumed by large files somewhere else (or deleted files held open by processes). The patch history hinted at a 'find / -xdev -type f -size +50M' diagnostic. I need to locate what's eating the space before I can install real curl.",
  "plan": "Find large files (>50M) and check for deleted-but-open files (lsof). Also check /var, /usr, and other mount points. Then clean up the culprit to free space, then retry apt-get install curl.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "find / -xdev -type f -size +50M -exec ls -lh {} \\; 2>/dev/null | sort -k5 -h | tail -n 20\n",
      "duration": 3.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "du -sh /var /usr /var/lib 2>/dev/null\n",
      "duration": 3.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Key discrepancy: `df` reports 959G used / 0 available (100% full), but `du` across /usr, /var, /root, etc. only accounts for ~250M, and no file >50M exists. This means the disk space is NOT consumed by visible files in the container — it's either (a) deleted files still held open by running processes, or (b) the overlay is backed by the host's disk which is full. I need to pinpoint the cause before apt can download the real curl. Let me inspect lsof for deleted-but-open files and a per-directory du.",
  "plan": "Run `lsof` filtered for deleted files (a common cause of df/du mismatch) and a top-level `du` to find where space is attributed. Also check inode usage. Once I find the space leak, I can free it (e.g., truncate/handle the held-open file or remove the actual culprit), then retry `apt-get install -y curl` to overwrite the fake script and restore real curl.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "du -sh /* 2>/dev/null | sort -h\n",
      "duration": 3.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "lsof 2>/dev/null | awk '/deleted/ {print $7, $1, $2, $NF}' | sort -n | tail -n 15\n",
      "duration": 2.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "df -i / 2>/dev/null\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    }
  ]
}

